Introduction | page 1
1. INTRODUCTION
This report is based on the Gilgit-Baltistan Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS), conducted in 2024-25 by the
Statistical Cell, Planning and Development (P&D) Department, Government of Gilgit -Baltistan (GB) with the
technical support from UNICEF Pakistan. The survey provides statistically sound and internationally comparable
data essential for developing evidence -based policies and programmes, and for monitoring progress toward
national goals and global commitments.
A Commitment to Action: National and International Reporting Responsibilities

--- page 21 ----------------------------------------------------
Baltistan: Skardu, Ghanche, Shigar and Kharmang
The samples for the households were selected in two stages. Within each stratum, a specified number of census
enumeration areas were selected systematically with probability proportional to size at the first stage.
After a fresh household listing was carried out within the selected enumeration areas, a systematic sample of
20 households was drawn in each sample enumeration area. The total sample size was 7,140 households in 357
clusters. All the selected 357 enumerat ion areas were visited during the fieldwork period. As the sample is not
self-weighting samples weights are used for reporting survey results. A more detailed description of the sample
design can be found in Appendix A: Sample Design.
2.3  CONSULTATIVE WORKSHOPS
Stakeholders’ consultative workshops were organized at Gilgit to get input from government line departments,
sector experts, academicians, and Civil Society Organizations (CSOs) on the proposed list of indicators. Detailed

